er the ph of this media  now all of these cell culture media  comes with  ph indicator such as phenol red  which is generally 7.1  and it looks red when it's all right  but the problem is when there is  exhaustion of the media or buildup of  lactic acid or any kind of metabolic  substances  the ph might fall dramatically to six  as a result we would understand  immediately  we have to subculture so let me give you  a quick idea  about some color changes and  possible interpretation so if ph  is 7 then everything is fine  seven to seven point four everything is  fine  now look at this situation when the  media is slightly orangish  so you need to change it within 24 hours  and it's okay  but when the media is media ph is 6.5  then immediate change is important  or there could be one contamination as  well when the ph  is 7.8 there might be some problem with  co2 supply so this  is kind of a thumb rule now  once we understand when to do a  subculture  now we can understand how to do a  subculture  so let's hop into the cell culture lab  now first we have to examine the cells  under the microscope  for its confluency we have to ensure  that there is no contamination and the  color change is not due to contamination  so once we have examined that part  visually we can decide that we have to  subculture right now  and all this process has to be done  under a sterile cell culture hood  so now let's hop into the sterile cell  culture hole  here you can first take out the media  which is now yellowish in color  after taking out this media you can  rinse the cells  with pbs and these cells are adhered to  the bottom of these  cell culture flask now if it's a  non-adherent cell  it would be slightly difficult then  generally people put trypsin  which would dislodge the cell from this  bottom  after putting trypsin you wait for some  time or incubate the cell for some time  and then you resuspend the cell  and try to take out the trypsinite cell  using this pipeline now  these cells which are trypsinized  are added to a media so this would block  the trypsin activity  and trypsin activity is not good for  cells  so obviously after uh blocking the  trypsin activity  we have to centrifuge it and obtain the  cell pellet  this media which contains some amount of  trypsin has to be thrown away  and fresh media has to be added  the cells need to be resuspended in this  fresh media  then some amount of cells need to be  seeded into a fresh  plus containing fresh media and this is  how  we perform subculture so i hope you  understand the subculturing technique  and these basic aspects of cell culture  now there is a cell culture playlist you  can watch all these videos  now let me tell you that you can also  support my channel  in patreon the link would be given in  the description  if you are indian viewer you can support  my channel  by donating some amount of money even  it's nominal  your donation matters a lot for our  channel  so you can use this qr code to pay me  and you can also take my courses in  unacademy which is india's biggest  learning platform  and please don't forget to like share  and subscribe  thank you
 

